course: biology
lesson: 278
title: Evolution: why giraffes really have long necks
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: An old idea said giraffes stretched their necks to reach high leaves and then passed the longer necks to their babies, but that is wrong: stretching your body does not change the DNA you pass on, just as a weightlifter's babies are not born with big muscles.
explain: The real answer: giraffe calves are born with slightly different neck lengths, and those with longer necks often got more food or won more fights, so they had more babies, who tended to have long necks too.
explain: Repeat that over thousands of generations and the average neck grows longer; this is natural selection, the idea of Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace.
explain: Evolution does not plan ahead or aim to be «better»: it just keeps whatever helps animals leave babies in their own place, which is why cave fish in the dark can lose their eyes.
explain: It also changes what is already there instead of starting fresh, so a giraffe has the same seven neck bones as you and a mouse, only much longer.
fact: In 1809 Jean-Baptiste Lamarck suggested that animals pass on body changes they gained by using them, such as a stretched neck; this is wrong, because stretching does not change the DNA passed on.
fact: Giraffes are born with slightly different neck lengths; those with longer necks often got more food or won more fights and left more babies, so over many generations the average neck grew longer.
fact: Male giraffes fight by swinging their heavy heads and necks at each other, which is called necking; males with longer, stronger necks win more often, so long necks may be about fighting as well as food.
fact: A giraffe has 7 neck bones, the same number as a mouse and a person; each bone is just very long, up to about 25 centimetres or more.
fact: The okapi, with a shorter neck and stripy legs, is the giraffe's closest living cousin; it lives in thick forest, where a short neck suits its way of life, and fossils show giraffe relatives with shorter necks.
fact: Charles Darwin sailed round the world on HMS Beagle from 1831 to 1836 and noticed animals on islands; he collected evidence for about 20 years before publishing On the Origin of Species in 1859.
fact: Alfred Russel Wallace came up with the same idea of natural selection at the same time as Darwin.
fact: Evolution means fitting the place you live, not getting better in general; a fish is not worse than a person, it is fitted to water, and if the place changes, the best fit changes too.
fact: Cave fish in Mexico live in total darkness and lost their eyes over many generations, because eyes cost energy and are useless in the dark; fish of the same species near the surface still have eyes.
fact: Evolution has no plan or goal; it is just which living things leave more babies in their time and place, and the result can look designed but happens step by step.
fact: Fossils show that early whale relatives such as Pakicetus were land mammals that walked on four legs about 50 million years ago; some whales still have tiny hip bones inside their bodies.
fact: The eye could evolve bit by bit: a light-sensing patch is already useful, a cup shape shows direction, a pinhole makes a picture, and a lens makes it sharp; all these stages exist in living animals today.
fact: There are no animals with wheels, because each step of evolution must work on its own and a wheel cannot be built step by step from living parts that need blood and nerves; bacteria do have tiny spinning motors.
exercise: Lamarck said giraffes stretched their necks and passed long necks to their babies. What is wrong with this? | Stretching your body does not change the DNA you pass on | Giraffes never eat leaves | Giraffes are born with long necks already fully grown to adult size | Lamarck said giraffes have short necks
exercise: Why are a weightlifter's children not born with big muscles? | Muscles built in life do not change the DNA passed on | Babies are too small for any muscles | Weightlifters only have daughters | Muscles disappear from the family after one year has gone by
exercise: What is the real reason giraffes' necks grew long? | Longer-necked giraffes often fed better or won fights and left more babies | Giraffes wished hard for long necks | Each giraffe stretched its neck a little every day and passed the stretch on | Giraffes ate a special plant that made necks grow
exercise: What is necking in giraffes? | Males fighting by swinging their heads and necks | Giraffes hugging their young babies with their necks | Giraffes sleeping with their long necks on the ground | Giraffes stretching their necks before a long run
exercise: How many neck bones does a giraffe have? | 7 | 70 | 17 | 2
exercise: Which living animal is the giraffe's closest cousin? | The okapi | The plains zebra | The Arabian camel | The wild horse
exercise: On which ship did Charles Darwin sail round the world? | HMS Beagle | The Titanic | The Mayflower | HMS Victory
exercise: In which year did Darwin publish On the Origin of Species? | 1859 | 1759 | 1959 | 1809
exercise: Who had the idea of natural selection at the same time as Darwin? | Alfred Russel Wallace | Isaac Newton | Jean-Baptiste Lamarck | Louis Pasteur
exercise: What does evolution really mean? | Fitting the place you live better | Always getting bigger and stronger | Becoming more and more like humans | Getting cleverer and cleverer every year
exercise: Why did the Mexican cave fish lose their eyes? | Eyes cost energy and are useless in total darkness | The fish poked them out on rocks | The water dissolved them | The fish closed their eyes for too long in the dark caves
exercise: Does evolution have a goal or plan? | No, it is just who leaves more babies in their time and place | Yes, to make every animal into a human in the end | Yes, to make animals bigger | Yes, to make every animal grow wings and fly one day
exercise: What was Pakicetus? | An early relative of whales that walked on four legs | A fish that climbed trees | The first giraffe | A flying reptile
exercise: What do some whales still have inside their bodies? | Tiny hip bones | Small feathers on the tail | Gills from fish | Fur under the blubber
exercise: How could an eye evolve bit by bit? | From a light patch to a cup, a pinhole and then a lens | It appeared all at once in one baby | It grew from an ear | It came from a bright shiny stone that the animal swallowed
exercise: Why are there no animals with wheels? | A wheel cannot be built step by step from living parts with blood and nerves | Wheels are far too heavy for animals to carry about on their legs and bodies | Animals once had wheels but lost them | No animal has ever needed to move fast
exercise: What spinning part do some bacteria have? | Tiny spinning motors | Tiny wheels with tyres | Tiny propellers made of metal | Tiny ticking clocks inside
test: «my teacher said giraffes stretched their necks for leaves and their babies got the long necks. that's how it works yeah?» What is right? | No, that is Lamarck's old idea; long-necked giraffes left more babies over many generations | Yes, stretching makes the babies' necks longer | Yes, but only for female giraffes | No, giraffe necks have never changed
test: Count them: a giraffe and a mouse stand side by side. How many neck bones do they have together? | 14 | 7 | 24 | 21
test: «a fish is less evolved than me cos it can't even walk lol» What is right? | No, a fish is fitted to water as well as you are fitted to land | Yes, fish are half-finished people | Yes, fish stopped evolving long ago | No, fish are more evolved than you because they have been around much longer
test: What would happen over many generations if a river fish species got trapped in a dark cave? | Its eyes might slowly get smaller or disappear | It would grow bigger eyes to see in the dark | It would turn into a bat | Nothing could ever change
test: «if giraffes needed long necks so bad, why's the okapi got a short one» What is the best answer? | The okapi lives in thick forest, where a short neck fits its way of life | The okapi is a baby giraffe | The okapi forgot to stretch | The okapi's neck was bitten short by lions, and its babies were born that way
test: In a cartoon, a giraffe ties its long neck in a knot and then untangles it. Why can't a real giraffe? | Its neck has only 7 long bones and bends like ours, not like rope | Its neck is made of rubber | Real giraffes have no bones in their necks, but their skin is far too thick | Real giraffes can tie two knots, not one
test: Count them: Darwin sees 3 giant tortoises and 2 iguanas on an island. How many legs in all? | 20 | 12 | 10 | 18
test: «Darwin did it all alone and was first» What is closer to the truth? | Wallace had the same idea of natural selection at the same time | Darwin copied all of it from Lamarck, who wrote it down years before | Darwin got it from a TV show | Nobody had the idea before 1959
test: «half an eye is useless so eyes can't evolve» What is right? | A simple light patch is already useful, and every stage between exists in animals today | Right, eyes came all at once | Right, only people have eyes | Eyes evolved from teeth
test: «evolution's goal is to make humans, we're the top» What is right? | Evolution has no goal; it keeps whatever helps animals leave babies in their place | Yes, every animal is trying to become human, and apes are just the closest so far | Yes, and dogs are next | Evolution aims to make everything fly
test: «a whale is a big fish, always lived in the sea» What does the evidence show? | Whales come from land mammals like Pakicetus that walked on four legs | Whales came from sharks | Whales came from giant squid that lost their arms and grew a tail and flippers | Whales have no relatives at all
exam: Identify the principal deficiency of the hypothesis advanced by Lamarck in respect of the neck of the giraffe. | Characteristics acquired during a lifetime are not transmitted through the DNA to offspring | Giraffes do not consume foliage of any kind | The necks of giraffes possess no bones | Giraffes produce no offspring
exam: State the number of cervical vertebrae, hereinafter referred to as neck bones, possessed by the giraffe. | Seven | Seventy | Seventeen | Two
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the purpose or goal of evolution. | Evolution possesses no purpose or goal whatsoever | Evolution is directed towards the production of humans | Evolution is directed towards ever greater size | Evolution is directed towards flight in all creatures
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the loss of eyes in Mexican cave fish? | Eyes, being costly and useless in darkness, diminished over many generations | Each individual fish elected to discard its eyes | The eyes were removed by predatory creatures | The fish were born without eyes in a single generation
